ler of Kishangarh in Rajasthan from 1748 and a poet who wrote devotional verse to Krishna under the name Nagari Das.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sawayama-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sawayama-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sawayama Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sawayama Castle was a Japanese mountain castle on Mount Sawa, in modern Hikone, Shiga Prefecture, commanding the road along the eastern side of Lake Biwa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sawdan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sawdan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sawdan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sawdan was the third and last emir of Bari, ruling from about 857 to 871.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sawlon"><span class="enc-name">Sawlon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sawlon was saopha of Mohnyin, a Shan state in northern Burma, in the early 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sawlu"><span class="enc-name">Sawlu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sawlu was king of Pagan from 1077 to 1084, the son and successor of Anawrahta.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/saxe-merseburg"><span class="enc-name">Saxe-Merseburg</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">Saxe-Merseburg was a duchy created in 1656–1657 for Christian, a younger son of Elector John George I of Saxony, and centred on Merseburg on the Saale.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/saxe-weissenfels.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Saxe-Weissenfels</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">Saxe-Weissenfels was a duchy created in 1656–1657 for Augustus, a younger son of Elector John George I of Saxony, with its residence at Weissenfels on the Saale from…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/saxnot"><span class="enc-name">Saxnot</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Saxnot was a god of the pagan Saxons, named with Thunaer and Woden in the Old Saxon baptismal vow of the late eighth or ninth century, which required converts to…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/saxo-grammaticus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/saxo-grammaticus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Saxo Grammaticus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Saxo Grammaticus was a Danish historian and cleric in the service of Archbishop Absalon of Lund.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/saxon-retablissement"><span class="enc-name">Saxon Rétablissement</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Rétablissement was the programme of reconstruction in the Electorate of Saxony after the Seven Years' War, begun in 1762–1763 under Elector Frederick Christian and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/saxon-wars.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Saxon Wars</span><span class="enc-type">War · 772–804</span><span class="enc-snip">The Saxon Wars were the campaigns fought from 772 to 804 by Charlemagne against the Saxons, a Germanic people living east of the Rhine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/saxons"><span class="enc-name">Saxons</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Saxons were a Germanic people of northern Germany,