lopedia/sayana"><span class="enc-name">Sayana</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayana was a Sanskrit scholar and minister at the court of the early Vijayanagara kings, especially Bukka I and Harihara II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayed-darwish"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sayed-darwish.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sayed Darwish</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayed Darwish was an Egyptian singer and composer from Alexandria, considered the father of Egyptian popular music, who set to music Bilady, Bilady, Bilady, the song…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla"><span class="enc-name">Sayf al-Dawla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayf al-Dawla was a Hamdanid prince who founded the Emirate of Aleppo in 945, while his brother Nasir al-Dawla held Mosul.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-din-suleyman-bey"><span class="enc-name">Sayf al-Din Suleyman Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayf al-Din Suleyman Bey was the founder of the Eshrefid beylik in central Anatolia, a descendant of the Turkmen commander Eshref.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-din-suri"><span class="enc-name">Sayf al-Din Suri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayf al-Din Suri was a prince of the Ghurid dynasty of Ghor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayf-ibn-dhi-yazan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sayf-ibn-dhi-yazan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan was a semi-legendary Himyarite king of Yemen, remembered in Arabic folk tradition.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sayfawa-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sayfawa dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sayfawa dynasty was the ruling house of Kanem and later Bornu around Lake Chad, which claimed descent from Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayin"><span class="enc-name">Sayin</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayin was the chief god of the ancient Kingdom of Hadramawt in southern Arabia, usually understood as a moon god.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayn-abbey"><span class="enc-name">Sayn Abbey</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayn Abbey is a former Premonstratensian monastery in Bendorf, near Koblenz, founded in 1202 by Count Henry II of Sayn.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayram"><span class="enc-name">Sayram</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayram is an ancient town in southern Kazakhstan, east of Shymkent, on the site usually identified with medieval Isfijab.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayri-tupac"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sayri-tupac.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sayri Túpac</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayri Túpac was ruler of the Neo-Inca State at Vilcabamba from 1545, after the death of his father Manco Inca.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-abdul-jabbar-shah"><span class="enc-name">Sayyid Abdul Jabbar Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sayyid Abdul Jabbar Shah was chosen as ruler of Swat by the Yusufzai tribes of the valley